 "report": "Structure found. 22 of the 30 columns are pure uniform noise; the structure lives in 8 columns, in two groups of four, and is only visible through mod-1 integer combinations: u=(2*x10+2*x23+x25+x30) mod 1 and v=(2*x1-2*x6-x11-x24) mod 1. On noise both are uniform on [0,1); on the data, (u,v) lies on a three-armed 'Y' (a 1-D branching skeleton): junction at about (0.517,0.511), three straight arms ending at about (0.607,0.542), (0.434,0.575) and (0.502,0.399) (directions ~19, ~142, ~-98 degrees, lengths ~0.10-0.12), points spread evenly along the arms with perpendicular scatter sd ~0.005. The shape sits near the middle of the unit square, so it does not cross the wrap-around border. How: marginals, correlations, PCA, pairwise/triple histograms and Fourier characters were all null. A scan of all 4-column subsets (chi-square histograms and torus nearest-neighbour distances) flagged exactly two subsets (x10,x23,x25,x30) and (x1,x6,x11,x24) at z~40-57 (all others |z|<5). A Fourier-character search over integer coefficients inside each gave the combinations (2,2,1,1) and (2,-2,-1,-1) with resultant length ~0.96; plotting one against the other revealed the Y. No third coordinate found: position along the Y is independent of other columns' low-order characters, and a nearest-neighbour scan of all other 5-column subsets was null (relations needing 6+ columns were not scanned). A fit on 4000 rows reproduces on the 1000 held-out rows."
